# DoorRepo's runtime working directory: the catalog cache and any archives a
# sysop downloaded through the door. Regenerated on demand; the .keep marker
# is tracked so the directory exists in a fresh checkout.
board/Doors/DoorRepo/DoorRepo.log
board/Doors/DoorRepo/downloads/*
!board/Doors/DoorRepo/downloads/.keep
# The per-launch bearer token the BBS mints and writes here every time a
# sysop runs the door (door-launch-token.ts / config_read_token() in the C
# door) - a live credential, never a fixture. Without this, the first
# launch after a fresh checkout puts a real token in the worktree.
board/Doors/DoorRepo/DoorRepo.token

# Python bytecode from dev/scripts helpers
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# What a sysop set on a door in the admin, secrets among them. The
# DECLARATION (Doors/<door>/door.settings.json) ships with the door; the
# VALUES never do - the deploy syncs a door's directory without deleting,
# so a file the image does not carry is what survives a deploy.
board/Doors/*/settings.json

# Test fixtures are real board bytes, and are kept.
#
# The rules above ignore User.data, User.keys and user.misc because a LIVE
# board's caller data must never be committed. A two-record excerpt that a
# test reads is a different thing, and without this the importer's user test
# passes for whoever created the fixture and fails for everyone else.
!web/backend/tests/fixtures/**

# A 68K emulator trace capture, 42 MB / 992,731 lines, written at the repo root
# by the XIM debug path. It was indexed as drawable art by the screen gallery
# and removed from the live board at the sysop's request; tracking it meant a
# deploy would put it back.
68klog.txt

